    {"text":"Bukhansan, which guards the northern part of Seoul like a folding screen, is a granite mountain that rose due to tectonic movements between the Jurassic and Cretaceous periods of the Mesozoic era, approximately 170 million years ago. Its highest peak, Baegundae (837 meters), along with Insubong (811 meters) to the north and Mangyeongdae (800 meters) to the south, form a triangular shape with their three horn-like peaks. For a long time, it was called Samgaksan (Three Peaks Mountain). After the Qing invasion of Joseon in 1636, Kim Sang-heon, an anti-Qing faction scholar, was taken to the Qing Dynasty in 1640. He lamented, “Farewell, Samgaksan, until we meet again, Han River.” From the late Joseon period, it was also called Bukhansan (Northern Han Mountain) because it lies north of Hanyang (old Seoul), a name that continues today.","decision":"translate"},
    {"text":"The Chosunilbo","decision":"preserve"},
    {"text":"시열라내나","decision":"preserve"}
]

